    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "uh, what do you mean by \"dead goblin\"? that's a weird way to put it. anyway, can we just stick to the math? i need to know how to actually calculate the total load so i don't mess up the beam sizing. how do i combine those two loads for the final spec?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the goblin slip-up earlier; I was just trying to illustrate that dead load is the constant, unyielding weight of the structure itself, like a goblin who refuses to move no matter how much you push them.\n\nTo get your beam sizing right for Vancouver, you need to combine the **Dead Load** (the weight of the Western Red Cedar framing, joists, and decking) with the **Live Load** (people, snow, and furniture). You do this by adding them together to get the **Total Uniform Load** (usually expressed in pounds per square foot, or psf).\n\nHere is the basic formula you'll use:\n**Total Load (psf) = Dead Load (psf) + Live Load (psf)**\n\nFor example, if your Western Red Cedar framing weighs 10 psf and the code requires a 40 psf live load for occupancy, your total design load is 50 psf. You then use this combined number to look up the required beam size and post spacing in your structural tables.\n\n**A quick note on Vancouver specifics:** Since you are in the Lower Mainland, you must also add the **Snow Load**. Vancouver has specific",
